search-and-rescue efforts are underway  in Southern California where deadly  mudslides wiped away dozens of homes  storms and mudflows killed at least 15  people in Santa Barbara County they're  concerned that the number could go much  higher more than 20 people are still  missing emergency crews say they  received more than 600 calls for help in  a three-hour period yesterday Carter  Evans is in hard-hit Montecito that is  about two hours northwest of Los Angeles  Carter good morning good morning so I  want you to imagine what this must have  been like it would have been about this  time yesterday morning about 4 o'clock  in the morning it's dark you're sound  asleep and then all of a sudden out of  nowhere this comes barreling down the  hill right toward your home take a look  at what it can do to a home when it  comes by you can see the front of this  home was ripped right off now the mud  was Unleashed early Tuesday due to a  combination of torrential rainfall and  recent wildfires in one area almost an  inch of rain fell in 15 minutes it  buried homes and roads in an area  scorched by the Thomas fire last month  now mudslides are common after a  wildfire because the trees and  vegetation that had held the soil in  place burned in the fire the heavy rains  loosens the soil and then it begins  sliding downhill now as the flow picks  up speed it also collects rocks and  trees and pushes them at up to 20 miles  per hour the debris damages and destroys  homes while the mud begins to harden now  once that happens the search of rescue  effort becomes very very difficult more  than 50 people had to be rescued by  helicopters including a family of five  and there are still many people who are  unaccounted for  so those rescues are going to continue  today  John thank you Carter
 

